From 91379b0e6b4cd27bc032a507e07860d5d2cbc70e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: R Chromium port <idumych@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 01:05:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] (R1/beta5 backport) kernel: add restrict_syscalls(), a per-team syscall filter

A team can give up groups of syscalls (BASIC, FILE_READ, FILE_WRITE,
SOCKET, PROCESS, PORT, AREA_SHARE, DEBUG, SYSTEM), irreversibly, for
itself and every team it creates afterwards. Denied syscalls return
B_NOT_ALLOWED. Haiku has nothing like Linux's seccomp or OpenBSD's
pledge(), and POSIX credentials do not cover ports, sockets or most
system-wide syscalls, so a sandboxed process (a browser renderer, for
one) could not otherwise be kept from messaging other applications.

- Groups, not syscall numbers, which change between builds. A table
  in syscall_filter.cpp names the syscalls of each group; any syscall
  it does not name, including ones added later, is SYSTEM (fail closed).
- In BASIC, syscalls that name a thread, semaphore, area or team work
  on the caller's own only; another team's needs SYSTEM (or PROCESS for
  the caller's children). Opening for writing needs FILE_WRITE, stat()
  of a path FILE_READ, ioctls other than FIONBIO/FIONREAD SYSTEM.
  Of the generic syscalls only getentropy() is BASIC, checked in
  _user_generic_syscall() on its own copy of the subsystem name.
- Enforced at every syscall entry: THREAD_FLAGS_SYSCALL_FILTERED takes
  a slow path in x86_64's native and compat (32 bit) entries, the
  latter being reachable from a 64 bit team, and syscall_dispatcher()
  checks for arm64 and riscv64. Elsewhere restrict_syscalls() returns
  B_NOT_SUPPORTED, so nothing believes it is filtered when it is not.
- src/tests/system/kernel/syscall_filter_test.cpp covers the groups,
  ownership checks, existing threads, waiting for threads that have
  already exited, fork() and load_image() inheritance, and that a
  restriction cannot be widened.

How this was tested: built with the tree's cross tools (jam
kernel_x86_64 libroot.so syscall_filter_test) and installed on Renku
nightly build00062 (aae7d9f7) x86_64 in VirtualBox, replacing only
kernel_x86_64 and libroot.so in the haiku package. It boots to the
desktop; syscall_filter_test passes all 9 cases, 10 runs out of 10.
R Chromium's renderers, restricted to BASIC and FILE_READ, run on it
and render pages; that run found getentropy() missing from BASIC.
Shutdown then panics in tcp's EndpointManager::Unbind, exactly as the
unmodified build00055 kernel does on the same VM (issue #167).
Not tested: the 32 bit compat entry from a 64 bit team, arm64, riscv64.

The change is additive: the new syscall is numbered last, the new Team
member is last, the thread flag is a free bit, and libroot only gains
restrict_syscalls().

